              highlighted: ["AB","BC","CE","BE"] },
            { text: "therefore the product of {GB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE}",
              visible: L2_GB,
              highlighted: ["GB","BC","CE"] },
            { text: "equals the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE}.",
              visible: L2_GB,
              highlighted: ["AB","BC","CE"] },
            { text: "And, if the common square on {CE} is subtracted, then it follows that {AB} equals {GB}, which is absurd.",
              visible: L2_GB,
              highlighted: ["AB","GB"] },
            { text: "Therefore the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} does not equal the square on {BE}.",
              visible: L2_BE,
              highlighted: ["AB","BC","CE","BE"] },
            { text: "I say next that neither is it less than the square on {BE}.",
              visible: L2_BE,
              highlighted: ["BE"] },
            { text: "For, if possible, let it equal the square on {BF},",
              visible: L2_F,
              highlighted: ["BF"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "F", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } },
            { text: "and let {HA|AH} be double {DF}.",
              visible: L2_H,
              highlighted: ["AH","DF"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "H", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } },
            { text: "Now it will again follow that {HC} is double {CF}, so that {CH|HC} is bisected at {F},",
              visible: L2_HC,
              highlighted: ["HC","CF","F"] },
            { text: "and for this reason the product of {HB} and {BC} together with the square on {FC|CF} equals the square on {BF}.",
              visible: L2_HB,
              highlighted: ["HB","BC","CF","BF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "HB·BC + FC² = BF²", ref: "II.6" }] },
            { text: "But, by hypothesis, the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} also equals the square on {BF}.",
              visible: L2_HB,
              highlighted: ["AB","BC","CE","BF"] },
            { text: "Thus the product of {HB} and {BC} together with the square on {CF} also equals",
              visible: L2_HB,
              highlighted: ["HB","BC","CF"] },
            { text: "the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE}, which is absurd.",
              visible: L2_HB,
              highlighted: ["AB","BC","CE"] },
            { text: "Therefore the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} is not less than the square on {BE}.",
              visible: L2_BE,
              highlighted: ["AB","BC","CE","BE"] },
            { text: "And it was proved that neither does it equal the square on {BE}.",
              visible: L2_BE,
              highlighted: ["BE"] },
            { text: "Therefore the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} is not square.",
              visible: L2_BE,
              highlighted: ["AB","BC","CE"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "Lemma 1") return "#lemma1";
            if (ref === "Lemma 2") return "#lemma2";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });
</script>
</figure>

I say then that the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} is not square.

If it is square, then it either equals the square on {BE}, or is less than the square on {BE}, but cannot be greater lest the unit be divided.

First, if possible, let the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} equal the square on {BE}, and let {GA|AG} be double the unit {DE}.

Since the whole {AC} is double the whole {CD}, and in them {AG} is double {DE}, therefore the remainder {GC} is also double the remainder {EC|CE}. Therefore {GC} is bisected by {E}.

Therefore the product of {GB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} equals the square on {BE}. [!just II.6]

But the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} also, by hypothesis, equals the square on {BE}, therefore the product of {GB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} equals the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE}.

And, if the common square on {CE} is subtracted, then it follows that {AB} equals {GB}, which is absurd.

Therefore the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} does not equal the square on {BE}.

I say next that neither is it less than the square on {BE}.

For, if possible, let it equal the square on {BF}, and let {HA|AH} be double {DF}.

Now it will again follow that {HC} is double {CF}, so that {CH|HC} is bisected at {F}, and for this reason the product of {HB} and {BC} together with the square on {FC|CF} equals the square on {BF}. [!just II.6]

But, by hypothesis, the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} also equals the square on {BF}.

Thus the product of {HB} and {BC} together with the square on {CF} also equals the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE}, which is absurd.

Therefore the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} is not less than the square on {BE}.

And it was proved that neither does it equal the square on {BE}.

Therefore the product of {AB} and {BC} together with the square on {CE} is not square.

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To find two rational straight lines commensurable in square only such that the square on the greater is greater than the square on the less by the square on a straight line commensurable in length with the greater.
----
proof:

Set out any rational straight line {AB:canvas_2}, and two square numbers {CD:canvas_2} and {DE:canvas_2} such that their difference {CE:canvas_2} is not square.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_2" width="260" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.29 diagram" src="propX29.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure: the rational line AB, the numbers CD (with E on it),
    // the semicircle AFB (semi) and the triangle ABF (tri), F placed so that
    // DC : CE = BA² : AF². Added, all invisible at rest: transparent
    // highlight targets AF, FB, DE, CE (;0;0;0); faceless, strokeless twins
    // semih / trih that carry the highlights of the filled semicircle and
    // triangle so a raised random face never covers the figure (#140); the
    // right angle at F (III.31), hidden at rest (showAngles is off).
    //
    // Statement slides stand over the full resting figure, lighting the
    // lines found. The walk then builds it: the semicircle sweeps, F
    // appears with the contrivance (AF lit), and the triangle outlines and
    // fills with "Join FB".
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A, B, C, D, E (lineSliders,
    // all set out in the first sentence) and the offscreen frees. F is
    // derived (first of a bichord) and listed per slide.
    var SET  = ["A","B","AB","C","D","E","CD","DE","CE"];
    var SEMI = SET.concat(["semi","semih"]);
    var CONT = SEMI.concat(["F","AF"]);
    var JOIN = CONT.concat(["FB","tri","trih"]);
    var RT   = JOIN.concat(["angF"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_2",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "X.29",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,130;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,130;0;0",
            "C0;point;free;-1000,160;0;0",
            "C3;point;free;1000,160;0;0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,30,0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,230,0",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "C;point;lineSlider;C0,C3,40,0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;C0,C3,200,0",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D;0;0;blue",
            "E;point;lineSlider;C0,C3,130,0",
            "M;point;midpoint;A,B;0;0",
            "ABF;circle;radius;M,A;0;0;0;0",
            "G;point;meanProportional;D,C,C,E,C,D;0;0",
            "F';point;proportion;D,C,C,G,A,B,A,B;0;0",
            "AFcirc;circle;radius;A,F';0;0;0;0",
            "FF'';line;bichord;ABF,AFcirc;0;0;0",
            "F;point;first;FF''",
            "semi;sector;arc;B,F,A;0;0;black;random",
            "tri;polygon;triangle;A,B,F;0;0;black;random",
            // Highlight targets and the marker only — invisible at rest.
            "AF;line;connect;A,F;0;0;0",
            "FB;line;connect;F,B;0;0;0",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
            "CE;line;connect;C,E;0;0;0",
            "semih;sector;arc;B,F,A;0;0;0;0",
            "trih;polygon;triangle;A,B,F;0;0;0;0",
            "angF;sector;angleMarkerRight;F,A,B"
        ],
        slides: [
            { text: "“To find two rational straight lines commensurable in square only",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "X.29" }] },
            { text: "such that the square on {the greater|AB} is greater than the square on {the less|AF}",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF"] },
            { text: "by the square on {a straight line|FB} commensurable in length with {the greater|AB}.”",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["FB","AB"] },
            { text: "Set out any rational straight line {AB},",
              visible: SET,
              highlighted: ["AB"] },
            { text: "and two square numbers {CD} and {DE}",
              visible: SET,
              highlighted: ["CD","DE"] },
            { text: "such that their difference {CE} is not square.",
              visible: SET,
              highlighted: ["CE"] },
            { text: "Describe the semicircle {AFB|semih} on {AB},",
              visible: SEMI,
              highlighted: ["AB"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "semi", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "and let it be contrived that {DC|CD} is to {CE} as the square on {BA|AB} is to the square on {AF}.",
              visible: CONT,
              highlighted: ["CD","CE","AB","AF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "DC : CE = BA² : AF²", ref: "X.6.Cor" }],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "F", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } },
            { text: "Join {FB}.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["FB"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "tri", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] } },
            { text: "Since the square on {BA|AB} is to the square on {AF} as {DC|CD} is to {CE},",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF","CD","CE"] },
            { text: "therefore the square on {BA|AB} has to the square on {AF} the ratio which the number {DC|CD} has to the number {CE}.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF","CD","CE"] },
            { text: "Therefore the square on {BA|AB} is commensurable with the square on {AF}.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "BA², AF² commensurable", ref: "X.6" }] },
            { text: "But the square on {AB} is rational, therefore the square on {AF} is also rational. Therefore {AF} is also rational.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AF rational", ref: "X.Def.I.4" }] },
            { text: "And, since {DC|CD} does not have to {CE} the ratio which a square number has to a square number,",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["CD","CE"] },
            { text: "neither does the square on {BA|AB} have to the square on {AF} the ratio which a square number has to a square number,",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF"] },
            { text: "therefore {AB} is incommensurable in length with {AF}.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AB, AF incommensurable in length", ref: "X.9" }] },
            { text: "Therefore {BA|AB} and {AF} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF"] },
            { text: "And since {DC|CD} is to {CE} as the square on {BA|AB} is to the square on {AF},",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["CD","CE","AB","AF"] },
            { text: "therefore, in conversion, {CD} is to {DE} as the square on {AB} is to the square on {BF|FB}.",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["CD","DE","AB","FB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "CD : DE = AB² : BF²", ref: "V.19.Cor" },
                               { claim: "angle AFB right", ref: "III.31" },
                               { claim: "AB² = AF² + FB²", ref: "I.47" }],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "But {CD} has to {DE} the ratio which a square number has to a square number,",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["CD","DE"] },
            { text: "therefore the square on {AB} has to the square on {BF|FB} the ratio which a square number has to a square number.",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["AB","FB"] },
            { text: "Therefore {AB} is commensurable in length with {BF|FB}.",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["AB","FB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AB, BF commensurable in length", ref: "X.9" }] },
            { text: "And the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {AF} and {FB},",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF","FB","angF"] },
            { text: "therefore the square on {AB} is greater than the square on {AF} by the square on {BF|FB} commensurable with {AB}.",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF","FB"] },
            { text: "Therefore there have been found two rational straight lines {BA|AB} and {AF} commensurable in square only",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF"] },
            { text: "such that the square on the greater {AB} is greater than the square on the less {AF}",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF"] },
            { text: "by the square on {BF|FB} commensurable in length with {AB}.",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["FB","AB"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "X.29") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });
</script>
</figure>

Describe the semicircle {AFB|semih} on {AB}, and let it be contrived that {DC|CD} is to {CE} as the square on {BA|AB} is to the square on {AF}. Join {FB}. [!just X.6.Cor]

Since the square on {BA|AB} is to the square on {AF} as {DC|CD} is to {CE}, therefore the square on {BA|AB} has to the square on {AF} the ratio which the number {DC|CD} has to the number {CE}. Therefore the square on {BA|AB} is commensurable with the square on {AF}. [!just X.6]

But the square on {AB} is rational, therefore the square on {AF} is also rational. Therefore {AF} is also rational. [!just X.Def.I.4]

And, since {DC|CD} does not have to {CE} the ratio which a square number has to a square number, neither does the square on {BA|AB} have to the square on {AF} the ratio which a square number has to a square number, therefore {AB} is incommensurable in length with {AF}. [!just X.9]

Therefore {BA|AB} and {AF} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only.

And since {DC|CD} is to {CE} as the square on {BA|AB} is to the square on {AF}, therefore, in conversion, {CD} is to {DE} as the square on {AB} is to the square on {BF|FB}. [!just V.19.Cor; III.31, I.47]

But {CD} has to {DE} the ratio which a square number has to a square number, therefore the square on {AB} has to the square on {BF|FB} the ratio which a square number has to a square number. Therefore {AB} is commensurable in length with {BF|FB}. [!just X.9]

And the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {AF} and {FB}, therefore the square on {AB} is greater than the square on {AF} by the square on {BF|FB} commensurable with {AB}.

Therefore there have been found two rational straight lines {BA|AB} and {AF} commensurable in square only such that the square on the greater {AB} is greater than the square on the less {AF} by the square on {BF|FB} commensurable in length with {AB}.

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
</div>
----
guide:

#### About Lemma 1

Euclid records in Lemma 1 a method to generate Pythagorean triples. As it depends only on the material in Book IX, logically, it could have appeared there rather than here in Book X. It appears here since it is needed in the proof of the proposition. It is likely much older than the rest of Book X.

Here is a paraphrase of the method of lemma 1, written in a modern algebraic notation. Let *n*<sup>2</sup> (= {AB:canvas_0}) and *m*<sup>2</sup> (= {BC:canvas_0}) be two numbers of the same parity with *n*<sup>2</sup> larger. Then *n*<sup>2</sup> – *m*<sup>2</sup> is an even number, so let *k* (= {CD:canvas_0}) be its half. Then

<center>
<i>n</i><sup>2</sup><i>m</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>k</i><sup>2</sup> = (<i>m</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>k</i> )<sup>2</sup>.
</center>

Therefore,

<center>
(<i>nm</i>)<sup>2</sup> + ((<i>n</i><sup>2</sup> – <i>m</i><sup>2</sup>)/2)<sup>2</sup> = ((<i>n</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>m</i><sup>2</sup>)/2)<sup>2</sup>,
</center>

which gives the two square numbers (*nm*)<sup>2</sup> and ((*n*<sup>2</sup> – *m*<sup>2</sup>)/2)<sup>2</sup> whose sum is also a square.

#### Pythagorean triples

Three numbers *x, y,* and *z* where the sum of squares of the first two is the square of the third form what is now called a *Pythagorean triple* *x* : *y* : *z.* This lemma shows that if *n* and *m* have the same parity and *n* > *m,* then *n* and *m* generate the Pythagorean triple

<center>
<i>nm</i> : (<i>n</i><sup>2</sup> – <i>m</i><sup>2</sup>)/2 : (<i>n</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>m</i><sup>2</sup>)/2
</center>

It is easy to see that if *x* : *y* : *z* is a Pythagorean triple, then so is any multiple *ax* : *ay* : *az* of it. A Pythagorean triple that is not a multiple of another Pythagorean triple is called a *primitive* Pythagorean triple. The numbers *n* and *m* are relatively prime if and only if the Pythagorean triple that they generate is primitive. Thus, when *n* and *m* are relatively prime odd numbers and *n* > *m,* then a primitive Pythagorean triple is generated.

Here's a table of a few small Pythagorean triples, indexed by *n* and *m.* Those that aren't primitive are colored with a gray background.

<center>
<table border="1" cellpadding="3" bgcolor="ffffff">
<tr align="center">
   <th></th><th>1</th><th>3</th><th>5</th><th>7</th><th>9</th>
   <th>11</th><th>13</th>
</tr>
<tr align="center"><th>3</th>
   <td>3 : 4 : 5</td>
</tr>
<tr align="center"><th>5</th>
   <td>5 : 12 : 13</td>
   <td>15 : 8 : 17</td>
</tr>
<tr align="center"><th>7</th>
   <td>7 : 24 : 25</td>
   <td>21 : 20 : 29</td>
   <td>35 : 12 : 37</td>
</tr>
<tr align="center"><th>9</th>
   <td>9 : 40 : 41</td>
   <td bgcolor="cccccc">27 : 36 : 45</td>
   <td>45 : 28 : 53</td>
   <td>63 : 16 : 65</td>
</tr>
<tr align="center"><th>11</th>
   <td>11 : 60 : 61</td>
   <td>33 : 56 : 65</td>
   <td>55 : 48 : 73</td>
   <td>77 : 36 : 85</td>
   <td>99 : 20 : 101</td>
</tr>
<tr align="center"><th>13</th>
